{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 1310,
    "dDurationMs": 4650,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The goal of encryption is to garble data is\nsuch a way so that no one who has the data"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5960,
    "dDurationMs": 1919,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "can read it unless they’re the intended\nrecipient."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 7879,
    "dDurationMs": 4051,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And the encryption of pretty much all private\ninformation sent over the internet relies"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 11930,
    "dDurationMs": 5030,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "immensely on one numerical phenomenon - as\nfar as we can tell, it’s really really hard"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 16960,
    "dDurationMs": 5500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to take a really big number and find its factors\nusing a normal, non-quantum computer."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 22460,
    "dDurationMs": 3790,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Unlike multiplication, which is very fast\n(just multiply the digits together and add"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 26250,
    "dDurationMs": 4189,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "them up ), finding the prime numbers that\nmultiply together to give you an arbitrary,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 30439,
    "dDurationMs": 4731,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "big, non-prime number appears to be slow - at\nleast, the best approach we currently have"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 35170,
    "dDurationMs": 4350,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that runs on a normal computer - even a very\npowerful one - is very slow."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 39520,
    "dDurationMs": 5140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Like, to find the factors of this number , it\ntook 2000 years of computer processor time!"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 44660,
    "dDurationMs": 4310,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now, it’s not yet proven that we won’t\neventually find a fast way to break encryption"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 48970,
    "dDurationMs": 4600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "just with normal computers, but it’s certain\nthat anybody with a large working quantum"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 53570,
    "dDurationMs": 5180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "computer today would pose an immediate privacy\nand security threat to the whole internet."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 58750,
    "dDurationMs": 2910,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And that’s due to something called “Shor’s\nAlgorithm.”"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 61660,
    "dDurationMs": 3930,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well actually it’s due to quantum superposition\nand interference; they’re just taken advantage"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 65590,
    "dDurationMs": 5550,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of by an algorithm developed by Peter Shor,\nwhich I’m now going to attempt to explain."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 71140,
    "dDurationMs": 5259,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The kind of encryption we’re talking about\ngarbles or “locks” messages using a large"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 76399,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "number in such a way that decrypting or “unlocking”\nthe data requires knowing the factors of that"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 81039,
    "dDurationMs": 4610,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "number . If somebody doesn’t have the factors,\neither they can’t decrypt the data, or they"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 85649,
    "dDurationMs": 4180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "have to spend a really really long time or\na huge amount of investment in computing resources"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 89829,
    "dDurationMs": 1600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "finding the factors."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 91429,
    "dDurationMs": 4000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Our current best methods essentially just\nguess a number that might be a factor, and"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 95429,
    "dDurationMs": 3491,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "check if it is . And if it isn’t, you try\nagain."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 98920,
    "dDurationMs": 1100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And again."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 100020,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And again."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 101020,
    "dDurationMs": 1389,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It’s slow."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 102409,
    "dDurationMs": 4681,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There are so many numbers to check that even\nthe fast clever ways to make really good guesses"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 107090,
    "dDurationMs": 1059,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "are slow."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 108149,
    "dDurationMs": 5301,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For example, my computer took almost 9 minutes\nto find the prime factors of this number."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 113450,
    "dDurationMs": 4599,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So if you used this number to encrypt your\ndata, it would only be safe from me for 9"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 118049,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "minutes."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 119049,
    "dDurationMs": 3691,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If, on the other hand, you used a number like\nthe one that took 2000 years of computer processor"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 122740,
    "dDurationMs": 4190,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "time to factor , your data would definitely\nbe safe from me and my laptop, but not from"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 126930,
    "dDurationMs": 4079,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "somebody with access to a server farm .\nThis is similar to how putting a lock on your"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 131009,
    "dDurationMs": 4370,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "door and bars on your windows doesn’t guarantee\nyou won’t have stuff stolen from your house,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 135379,
    "dDurationMs": 2861,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but does make it take more time and more work."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 138240,
    "dDurationMs": 4520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Encrypting data isn’t a guarantee of protection\n- it’s a way of making it harder to access;"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 142760,
    "dDurationMs": 3119,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "hopefully enough harder that no one thinks\nit’s worth trying."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 145879,
    "dDurationMs": 5081,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But quantum computation has the potential\nto make it super super easy to access encrypted"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 150960,
    "dDurationMs": 4330,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "data - like having a lightsaber you can use\nto cut through any lock or barrier, no matter"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 155290,
    "dDurationMs": 1240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "how strong."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 156530,
    "dDurationMs": 3190,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Shor’s algorithm is that lightsaber."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 159720,
    "dDurationMs": 3500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Roughly speaking, to factor a given number\nShor’s algorithm starts with a random crappy"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 163220,
    "dDurationMs": 4170,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "guess that might share a factor with your\ntarget number, (but which probably doesn’t),"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 167390,
    "dDurationMs": 3800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and then the algorithm transforms it into\na much better guess that probably DOES share"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 171190,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a factor!"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 172190,
    "dDurationMs": 3920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There’s nothing intrinsically quantum mechanical\nabout this - you can, in fact, run a version"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 176110,
    "dDurationMs": 4660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of Shor’s algorithm on a regular computer\nto factor big numbers, but perhaps unsurprisingly"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 180770,
    "dDurationMs": 4070,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the “turning your bad guess into a better\nguess” part of the process takes a very"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 184840,
    "dDurationMs": 2410,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "very long time on a normal computer."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 187250,
    "dDurationMs": 4799,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "On the other hand, this key step happens to\nbe ridiculously fast on quantum computers."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 192049,
    "dDurationMs": 4991,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So, our task is to explain how Shor’s algorithm\nturns a crappy guess into a better guess (which"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 197040,
    "dDurationMs": 4740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is purely mathematics), and why quantum computers\nmake that fast (which is where the physics"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 201780,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "comes in)."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 202780,
    "dDurationMs": 4110,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It all starts with a big number, N, that you’ll\nneed to find the factors of to break into"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 206890,
    "dDurationMs": 1319,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "some encrypted data."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 208209,
    "dDurationMs": 3980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If you don’t know what the factors are (which\nyou don’t), you can make a guess; just pick"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 212189,
    "dDurationMs": 4250,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "some number g that’s less than N . We actually\ndon’t need the guess to be a pure factor"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 216439,
    "dDurationMs": 3970,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of N - it could also be a number that shares\nsome factors with N, like how 4 isn’t a"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 220409,
    "dDurationMs": 2300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "factor of 6, but shares a factor with it."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 222709,
    "dDurationMs": 3221,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Numbers that share factors are ok because\nthere’s a two-thousand-year-old method to"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 225930,
    "dDurationMs": 4199,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "check for and find common factors - it’s\ncalled Euclid’s algorithm and it’s pretty"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 230129,
    "dDurationMs": 1001,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "darn efficient."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 231130,
    "dDurationMs": 4919,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "All this is to say that to find a factor of\nN, we don't have to guess a factor of N - guessing"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 236049,
    "dDurationMs": 4190,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "numbers that share factors with N works, too,\nthanks to Euclid."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 240239,
    "dDurationMs": 4161,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And if Euclid’s algorithm found any shared\nfactors with N, then we’d be done!"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 244400,
    "dDurationMs": 4410,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "You could just divide N by that factor to\nget the other factor and break the encryption."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 248810,
    "dDurationMs": 4629,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But for the big numbers used in encryption,\nit’s astronomically unlikely that any single"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 253439,
    "dDurationMs": 2091,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "guess will actually share a factor with N."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 255530,
    "dDurationMs": 4840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Instead, we’ll use a trick to help transform\nyour crappy guess into a pair of guesses that"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 260370,
    "dDurationMs": 4930,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "are way more likely to share factors with\nN. The trick is based on a simple mathematical"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 265300,
    "dDurationMs": 4690,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "fact: for any pair of whole numbers that don’t\nshare a factor, if you multiply one of them"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 269990,
    "dDurationMs": 4061,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "by itself enough times, you’ll eventually\narrive at some whole number multiple of the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 274051,
    "dDurationMs": 5759,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "other number, plus 1 . That is, if a and b\nare integers that don’t share factors, then"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 279810,
    "dDurationMs": 7370,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "eventually a^p will be equal to m times b\n+ 1, for some power p and some multiple m"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 287180,
    "dDurationMs": 4049,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": ". We don't have the time to get into why this\nis true, but hopefully a few illustrations"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 291229,
    "dDurationMs": 1801,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "can at least give you a feeling for it."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 293030,
    "dDurationMs": 2110,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For example, 7 and 15."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 295140,
    "dDurationMs": 4499,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "While seven squared isn’t one more than\na multiple of 15, and neither is seven cubed,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 299639,
    "dDurationMs": 1851,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "seven to the fourth is."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 301490,
    "dDurationMs": 5730,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Or take 42 and 13 - 42 squared isn’t one\nmore than a multiple of 13 , but 42 cubed"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 307220,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 308220,
    "dDurationMs": 3020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This same kind of thing works for any pair\nof numbers that don't share factors, though"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 311240,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the power p might be ridiculously large."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 312240,
    "dDurationMs": 4540,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So, for the big number, N, and your crappy\nguess, g, we’re guaranteed that some power"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 316780,
    "dDurationMs": 5780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of g is equal to some multiple of N, plus\n1 . And here’s the clever part - if we rearrange"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 322560,
    "dDurationMs": 9390,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "this equation by subtracting the 1 from both\nsides, we can rewrite g^p-1 as (g^p/2 + 1)(g^p/2"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 331950,
    "dDurationMs": 4380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "- 1) . You can multiply that back together\nto convince yourself that it works."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 336330,
    "dDurationMs": 3930,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And now we have an equation that almost looks\nlike “something” times “something”"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 340260,
    "dDurationMs": 4710,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is equal to N, which is exactly what we’re\ntrying to find - factors of N!"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 344970,
    "dDurationMs": 5379,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "These two terms are precisely the new and\nimproved guesses that Shor’s algorithm prescribes:"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 350349,
    "dDurationMs": 5731,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "take the initial crappy guess, multiply it\nby itself p/2 times, and either add or subtract"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 356080,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "one!"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 357080,
    "dDurationMs": 3440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Of course, since we’re dealing with a multiple\nof N rather than N itself, the terms on the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 360520,
    "dDurationMs": 4609,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "left hand side might be multiples of factors\nof N, rather than the factors themselves."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 365129,
    "dDurationMs": 9561,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Like how 7^4/2+1 = 50, and 7^4/2-1 = 48, neither\nof which is a factor of 15."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 374690,
    "dDurationMs": 4949,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But we can find shared factors by using Euclid’s\nalgorithm again, and once we do, we’ll have"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 379639,
    "dDurationMs": 1601,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "broken the encryption!"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 381240,
    "dDurationMs": 2120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So is this all Shor’s algorithm is?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 383360,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Where’s the quantum mechanics?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 384360,
    "dDurationMs": 2250,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Why can’t we use this to break encryption\nright now?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 386610,
    "dDurationMs": 5559,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well, indeed, there are three problems with\nthese new and improved guesses."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 392169,
    "dDurationMs": 3991,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "First, one of the new guesses might itself\nbe a multiple of N, in which case the other"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 396160,
    "dDurationMs": 4129,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "would be a factor of m and neither would be\nuseful to us in any way."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 400289,
    "dDurationMs": 5061,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And second, the power “p” might be an\nodd number , in which case p/2 isn’t a whole"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 405350,
    "dDurationMs": 4319,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "number and so our guess taken to the power\nof p/2 probably isn’t a whole number either,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 409669,
    "dDurationMs": 1741,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which is no good."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 411410,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "However, for a random starting guess, it turns\nout that at least 3/8ths of the time neither"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 416610,
    "dDurationMs": 6309,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of these problems happens and p does generate\nguesses that share factors with N and break"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 422919,
    "dDurationMs": 1351,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the encryption!"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 424270,
    "dDurationMs": 5149,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is worth repeating - for ANY initial\nguess that we make, at least 37.5% of the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 429419,
    "dDurationMs": 6810,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "time g^p/2 ±1 will lead to a factor of N,\ndecrypting the garbled message."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 436229,
    "dDurationMs": 4781,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Which means we’re 99% likely to break the\nencryption with fewer than 10 guesses."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 441010,
    "dDurationMs": 2740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "However, problem number three is the big one."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 443750,
    "dDurationMs": 4401,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Remember, to turn a crappy guess into a good\nguess we need to know how many times you have"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 448151,
    "dDurationMs": 4229,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to multiply our guess by itself before we\nget a multiple of N, plus 1."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 452380,
    "dDurationMs": 4939,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And for a normal computer, the act of finding\nthat power p takes a ton of work and time."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 457319,
    "dDurationMs": 4461,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It’s not hard for small numbers like 42\nand 13, but if our big number is a thousand"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 461780,
    "dDurationMs": 5109,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "digits long, and our crappy guess is 500 digits\nlong, then trying to figure out how many times"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 466889,
    "dDurationMs": 3650,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you have to multiply our guess by itself before\nyou get some multiple of the big number, plus"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 470539,
    "dDurationMs": 4291,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "one, takes a ridiculous amount of trial and\nerror on a normal computer - more effort than"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 474830,
    "dDurationMs": 3709,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it would have taken to just factor N by brute\nforce in the first place!"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 478539,
    "dDurationMs": 5941,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So finally, this is where quantum mechanics\ncomes in and speeds things up an INSANE amount."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 484480,
    "dDurationMs": 4690,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Unlike a normal computation which gives only\none answer for a given input, a quantum computation"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 489170,
    "dDurationMs": 4809,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "can simultaneously calculate a bunch of possible\nanswers for a single input by using a quantum"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 493979,
    "dDurationMs": 4660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "superposition - but you only get one of the\nanswers out at the end, randomly, with different"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 498639,
    "dDurationMs": 1590,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "probabilities for each one."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 500229,
    "dDurationMs": 5340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The key behind fast quantum computations is\nto set up a quantum superposition that calculates"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 505569,
    "dDurationMs": 4410,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "all possible answers at once while being cleverly\narranged so that all of the wrong answers"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 509979,
    "dDurationMs": 1881,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "destructively interfere with each other."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 511860,
    "dDurationMs": 3580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That way when you actually measure the output\nof the calculation, the result of your measurement"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 515440,
    "dDurationMs": 2080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is most likely the right answer."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 517520,
    "dDurationMs": 3420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In general it can be really hard to figure\nout how to put any particular problem into"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 520940,
    "dDurationMs": 4030,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a quantum form where all the wrong answers\ndestructively interfere, but that’s what"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 524970,
    "dDurationMs": 3880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Shor’s algorithm does for the problem of\nfactoring large numbers - well, actually,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 528850,
    "dDurationMs": 2120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it does it for the problem of finding the\npower “p”."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 530970,
    "dDurationMs": 4670,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Remember, at this point we've made a crappy\nguess g, and we're trying to find the power"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 535640,
    "dDurationMs": 7400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "p so that g to the p is one more than a multiple\nof N. A p that does that also means that g^p/2"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 543040,
    "dDurationMs": 4840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "±1 is very likely to share factors with N."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 547880,
    "dDurationMs": 3699,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So to begin the quantum computation, we need\nto set up a quantum mechanical computer that"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 551579,
    "dDurationMs": 3911,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "takes a number x as input, and raises our\nguess to the power of x."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 555490,
    "dDurationMs": 4490,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For reasons we'll see later, we need to keep\ntrack of both the number x, and our guess"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 559980,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to that power."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 560980,
    "dDurationMs": 3760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The computer then needs to take that result\nand calculate how much bigger than a multiple"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 564740,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of N it is."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 565740,
    "dDurationMs": 3550,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We'll call that the \"remainder\", and we'll\nwrite it as plus “something\" for whatever"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 569290,
    "dDurationMs": 4000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "something the remainder is (remember, we want\na remainder of 1)."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 573290,
    "dDurationMs": 2320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So far, no different from a normal computer."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 575610,
    "dDurationMs": 3599,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But since it’s a quantum computer, we can\nsend in a superposition of numbers and the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 579209,
    "dDurationMs": 4201,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "computation will be done simultaneously on\nall of them, first resulting in a superposition"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 583410,
    "dDurationMs": 3700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for each p of all possible powers our guess\ncould be raised to , and then a superposition"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 587110,
    "dDurationMs": 3500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for each p of how much bigger each of those\npowers are than a multiple of N."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 590610,
    "dDurationMs": 3630,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We can’t just measure this superposition\nto get the answer - if we did, we’d get"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 594240,
    "dDurationMs": 4500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a single random element of the superposition\nas output, like “our guess squared is 5"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 598740,
    "dDurationMs": 3969,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "more than a multiple of N” . Which is no\nbetter than just randomly guessing powers,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 602709,
    "dDurationMs": 1631,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which we can do with a normal computer."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 604340,
    "dDurationMs": 5170,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "No, we need to do something clever to get\nall the non-p answers to destructively interfere"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 609510,
    "dDurationMs": 4440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and cancel out, leaving us with only one possible\nanswer: p."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 613950,
    "dDurationMs": 3970,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Which it turns out we can do, based on another\nmathematical observation."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 617920,
    "dDurationMs": 4090,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This mathematical observation isn’t particularly\ncomplicated, but it is a tad subtle and it"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 622010,
    "dDurationMs": 2550,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "may not be immediately clear why we care."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 624560,
    "dDurationMs": 4760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "However, it’s the key idea that allows us\nto turn the problem of finding p into one"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 629320,
    "dDurationMs": 3620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that works well on a quantum computer, and\nso in some ways it’s the crux of Shor’s"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 632940,
    "dDurationMs": 2730,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "algorithm - which is to say, it’s worth\nthe effort!"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 635670,
    "dDurationMs": 4530,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Ok, so remember that IF we knew what p was,\nwe could raise our guess to that power and"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 640200,
    "dDurationMs": 3850,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "get one more than a multiple of N. On the\nother hand, if we take our guess to a random"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 644050,
    "dDurationMs": 4440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "power , it’s probably going to be some other\nnumber more than a multiple of N - say, 3"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 648490,
    "dDurationMs": 4909,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "more . But check this out - if we raise our\nguess to that random power plus p, it’s"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 653399,
    "dDurationMs": 3861,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "again 3 more than a multiple of N . If we\nraise our guess to that random power plus"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 657260,
    "dDurationMs": 4510,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "2 p, it’s again 3 more than a multiple of\nN. And so on."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 661770,
    "dDurationMs": 3379,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It’s pretty straightforward to show why\nthis works by multiplying out “something"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 665149,
    "dDurationMs": 4810,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "times N plus 1” with “something else times\nN plus 3”; you get “a different something"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 669959,
    "dDurationMs": 5761,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "times N, again plus 3” . And this works\nfor any power x - if g^x is r more than a"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 675720,
    "dDurationMs": 5820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "multiple of N, then g^(x+p) will also be r\nmore than a multiple of N (though a different"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 681540,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "multiple)."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 682540,
    "dDurationMs": 3500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So the power p that we’re looking for - the\none that allows us to improve our crappy guess"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 686040,
    "dDurationMs": 4030,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and find factors of N and break encryption\n- it has a repeating property where if we"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 690070,
    "dDurationMs": 4380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "take another power and add (or subtract) p\nto it, the amount more than a multiple of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 694450,
    "dDurationMs": 1650,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "N stays the same."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 696100,
    "dDurationMs": 3239,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This repeating property isn’t something\nyou could figure out from taking our guess"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 699339,
    "dDurationMs": 4461,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to just one power - it’s a structural relationship\nbetween different powers, and we can take"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 703800,
    "dDurationMs": 4050,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "advantage of it since quantum computations\ncan be performed on superpositions of different"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 707850,
    "dDurationMs": 1109,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "possible powers."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 708959,
    "dDurationMs": 4391,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Specifically, if we take the superposition\nof all possible powers and JUST measure the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 713350,
    "dDurationMs": 4310,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "“amount more than a multiple of N“ part,\nthen we’ll randomly get one of the possible"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 717660,
    "dDurationMs": 3600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "“amounts more than a multiple of N” as\nthe output - say, 3."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 721260,
    "dDurationMs": 3269,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The specific number doesn’t matter to us,\nbut what does matter is that this means we"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 724529,
    "dDurationMs": 4490,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "must be left with a superposition of purely\nthe powers that could have resulted in a remainder"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 729019,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of 3."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 730019,
    "dDurationMs": 4141,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is one of the special properties of quantum\ncomputation - if you put in a superposition"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 734160,
    "dDurationMs": 3549,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and get an answer that could have come from\nmore than one element of the superposition,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 737709,
    "dDurationMs": 3111,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "then you'll be left with a superposition of\njust those elements!"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 740820,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And in our case, because of the repeating\nproperty, those powers are all numbers that"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 745220,
    "dDurationMs": 1510,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "are “p” apart from each other."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 746730,
    "dDurationMs": 4260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "To recap, we’re trying to find p because\nit will allow us to turn our crappy guess"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 750990,
    "dDurationMs": 3770,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "into a good guess for a number that shares\nfactors with N, which will allow us to break"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 754760,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the encryption."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 755760,
    "dDurationMs": 4139,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And we now have a quantum superposition of\nnumbers that repeat periodically with a period"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 759899,
    "dDurationMs": 5801,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of p, or equivalently, they repeat with a\nfrequency of 1/p . If we can find the frequency,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 765700,
    "dDurationMs": 2080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we can find p and break the encryption!"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 767780,
    "dDurationMs": 5130,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And the best tool to find the frequencies\nof things is called a Fourier transform."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 772910,
    "dDurationMs": 4280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Fourier transforms are what allow you to input\nan audio signal as a wave and convert it into"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 777190,
    "dDurationMs": 3339,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a graph showing the different frequencies\nthat the wave is made up of."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 780529,
    "dDurationMs": 4170,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And there’s a quantum version of the Fourier\ntransform, which we can apply to our superposition"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 784699,
    "dDurationMs": 4741,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that repeats with a frequency of 1/p to cause\nall the different possible wrong frequencies"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 789440,
    "dDurationMs": 9930,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to destructively interfere, leaving us with\na single quantum state: the number 1/p."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 799370,
    "dDurationMs": 2740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So how does the quantum Fourier transform\nperform this magic?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 802110,
    "dDurationMs": 3520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well, if you input a single number into the\nquantum Fourier transform, it will give you"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 805630,
    "dDurationMs": 4010,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a superposition of all other numbers - but\nnot any old superposition."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 809640,
    "dDurationMs": 3480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "A superposition where the other numbers are\nall weighted by different amounts, and those"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 813120,
    "dDurationMs": 3460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "weights look roughly like a sine wave with\nthe frequency of the single number we put"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 816580,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 817580,
    "dDurationMs": 3640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If you put in a higher number, you get a sine\nwave-style superposition of all other numbers,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 821220,
    "dDurationMs": 1870,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but with a higher frequency."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 823090,
    "dDurationMs": 4470,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And the magic is that when you put IN a superposition\nof numbers, you get out a superposition of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 827560,
    "dDurationMs": 4620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "superpositions and the sine waves add together\n- or subtract and cancel out."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 832180,
    "dDurationMs": 3610,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And it happens that if you put in a superposition\nof numbers that are all separated by an amount"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 835790,
    "dDurationMs": 3820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "p, all those sine waves interfere so that\nwhat you get out (and I’m oversimplifying"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 839610,
    "dDurationMs": 4020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a touch), is the single quantum state representing\n1/p."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 843630,
    "dDurationMs": 5569,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Which we can finally measure to get the output\nof the computation: 1/p!"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 849199,
    "dDurationMs": 4841,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Which we invert to find p, and as long as\np is even we can now finally raise our guess"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 854040,
    "dDurationMs": 3799,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to the power p over two and add or subtract\none, and as long as we don’t get an exact"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 857839,
    "dDurationMs": 4871,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "multiple of N, we are guaranteed to have a\nnumber that shares factors with N. And therefore"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 862710,
    "dDurationMs": 4590,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we can use Euclid’s algorithm to quickly\nfind those factors, and thus we can finally"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 867300,
    "dDurationMs": 3039,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "take the encrypted data and decrypt it."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 870339,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And thus we will have broken the encryption."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 871339,
    "dDurationMs": 3521,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And that is Shor’s algorithm - the lightsaber\nthat can be used to cut through locks on the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 874860,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "internet."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 875860,
    "dDurationMs": 4140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "As complicated as this clearly is in practice...\n(and we've glossed over a ton of details),"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 880000,
    "dDurationMs": 4820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it’s surprising to me how simple the core\nstructure of Shor's algorithm actually is:"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 884820,
    "dDurationMs": 4930,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for any crappy guess at a number that shares\nfactors with N, that guess to the power p/2"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 889750,
    "dDurationMs": 4250,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "plus or minus one is a much much better guess,\nif we can find p."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 894000,
    "dDurationMs": 5790,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And we CAN find p almost immediately with\na single (if complex) quantum computation."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 899790,
    "dDurationMs": 4470,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "A normal computer would have to go one by\none through all possible powers, which would"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 904260,
    "dDurationMs": 4290,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "take an incredible amount of time for any\nreally really big number like the ones used"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 908550,
    "dDurationMs": 5469,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in encryption, since p could be almost any\nnumber up to N. The quantum version is ridiculously"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 914019,
    "dDurationMs": 5320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "ridiculously faster, and if a big enough quantum\ncomputer is ever built, then Shor’s algorithm"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 919339,
    "dDurationMs": 4421,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "would allow the user to very easily decrypt\nany data encrypted with a large-number factoring"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 923760,
    "dDurationMs": 3770,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "based system - which would pretty much ruin\nthe entire internet."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 927530,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "At this point, however, the biggest actual\nquantum implementations of Shor’s algorithm"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 932250,
    "dDurationMs": 3649,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "don’t have enough memory to hold more than\na few bits, which only allows factoring of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 935899,
    "dDurationMs": 6321,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "numbers like 15, 21, and 35 . Now, there are\nother methods of factoring using quantum computations"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 942220,
    "dDurationMs": 3970,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that are a bit more advanced, and have factored\nnumbers as big as a few hundred thousand using"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 946190,
    "dDurationMs": 4910,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "just a few quantum bits of memory . But they\nwould still need 2000 times more quantum memory"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 951100,
    "dDurationMs": 4510,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to factor even some of the smaller of the\nreally big numbers used in modern encryption"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 955610,
    "dDurationMs": 6140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": ". So, no need to worry about quantum computers\njust yet."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 961750,
    "dDurationMs": 4270,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If all this talk of breaking encryption makes\nyou a bit nervous and worried about your online"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 966020,
    "dDurationMs": 3689,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "safety, well, there’s something you can\ndo to improve your internet security right"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 969709,
    "dDurationMs": 4060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "now - I’ve been a long time user of the\npassword manager Dashlane who are sponsoring"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 973769,
    "dDurationMs": 4430,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "this video, and if you’ve never used a password\nmanager before, Dashlane is amazing."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 978199,
    "dDurationMs": 4010,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It generates and remembers a long, unique\npassword for each site or service that I use"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 982209,
    "dDurationMs": 2721,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "so that I don’t have to worry about remembering\npasswords; and of course all of my data and"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 984930,
    "dDurationMs": 3430,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "passwords are stored encrypted with very very\nlarge numbers."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 988360,
    "dDurationMs": 3169,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And Dashlane is more than just a password\nmanager - it lets you know when your passwords"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 991529,
    "dDurationMs": 3411,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "are old or weak or when a site or app you\nuse has been hacked so you can change your"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 994940,
    "dDurationMs": 4269,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "passwords, it encrypts and lets you securely\nshare passwords with family and coworkers,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 999209,
    "dDurationMs": 3391,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it can be used to securely store or share\nyour address, credit card info, and banking"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1002600,
    "dDurationMs": 3489,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "info, with just the people and sites you want\nto, it can be used as a VPN, and more."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1006089,
    "dDurationMs": 6110,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Oh, and Dashlane uses 2048 bit numbers for\nits encryption - numbers that big are estimated"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1012199,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to take a trillion times more effort to factor\nthan any that have so far been factored by"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1016519,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "brute force."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1017519,
    "dDurationMs": 3981,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And of course Dashlane is free for up to 50\npasswords for as long as you like, so you"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1021500,
    "dDurationMs": 1530,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "have nothing to lose checking it out."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1023030,
    "dDurationMs": 4040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But, if you want the very useful features\nof unlimited passwords, encrypted syncing"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1027070,
    "dDurationMs": 5100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of passwords, VPN, remote account access,\nand more, the first 200 people get 10% off"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1032170,
    "dDurationMs": 4920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Dashlane premium by going to dashlane.com/minutephysics\nand using promo code minutephysics."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1037090,
    "dDurationMs": 4130,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Again, that’s dashlane.com/minutephysics\nwith promo code minutephysics to simplify"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1041220,
    "dDurationMs": 1880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and encrypt your online life."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1043100,
    "dDurationMs": 3920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Dashlane has legitimately improved my online\nsecurity and changed my password habits for"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1047020,
